## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of closures, per-domain rate limiting, and Promise deduplication—common in real apps with multiple data sources.

## Key Concepts
- Per-domain rate limiting (token bucket)
- In-flight deduplication of identical URLs via shared Promise
- TTL-based caching with automatic refresh
- Closure-based state encapsulation for domain data
- Robust error handling and cancellation considerations

## Code Example
```javascript
class RateLimitedFetcher {
  constructor(maxPerSec = 4, ttl = 30000) {
    this.maxPerSec = maxPerSec;
    this.ttl = ttl;
    this.domainBuckets = new Map(); // domain -> { tokens, queue }
    this.cache = new Map(); // url -> { data, expiry }
    this.inFlight = new Map(); // url -> Promise
  }
  _domain(domain) {
    if (!this.domainBuckets.has(domain)) {
      this.domainBuckets.set(domain, { tokens: maxPerSec, queue: [] });
    }
    return this.domainBuckets.get(domain);
  }
  async fetch(url, init) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const cached = this.cache.get(url);
    if (cached && cached.expiry > now) return cached.data;
    if (this.inFlight.has(url)) return this.inFlight.get(url);
    const domain = new URL(url).host;
    const bucket = this._domain(domain);
    const attempt = () => {
      if (bucket.tokens > 0) {
        bucket.tokens--;
        return true;
      }
      return false;
    };
    const run = async () => {
      try {
        const res = await fetch(url, init);
        const data = await res.clone().json().catch(() => res.text());
        this.cache.set(url, { data, expiry: Date.now() + this.ttl });
        return data;
      } finally {
        bucket.tokens++;
        // drain queued requests if any
        if (bucket.queue.length) {
          const next = bucket.queue.shift();
          next();
        }
      }
    };
    const promise = (async () => {
      if (!attempt()) {
        await new Promise(r => bucket.queue.push(r));
      }
      return run();
    })();
    this.inFlight.set(url, promise);
    const result = await promise;
    this.inFlight.delete(url);
    return result;
  }
  invalidate(url) { this.cache.delete(url); }
  warmRefresh(url) { this.invalidate(url); return this.fetch(url); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you unit-test this under bursty traffic and verify per-domain quotas?
- How would you extend to prioritize certain domains or implement exponential backoff on failures?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates advanced JavaScript patterns including per-key caching, Promise deduplication, TTL-based eviction, and reactive update patterns commonly needed in frontend applications.

## Key Concepts
- **Closures and per-key maps**: Isolated state management for each cached key
- **Promise deduplication**: Avoiding redundant network requests for concurrent loads of the same key
- **TTL-based eviction**: Time-to-live expiration for cache entries
- **Change notifications**: Listener pattern for broadcasting data updates
- **Error handling**: Graceful handling of fetch failures and cache misses

## Code Example
```javascript
function createDataLoader(fetchFn, ttlMs) {
  const cache = new Map();

  function getEntry(key) {
    if (!cache.has(key)) {
      cache.set(key, { promise: null, data: null, expiry: 0, listeners: [] });
    }
    return cache.get(key);
  }

  const notify = (key, data, error) => {
    const entry = cache.get(key);
    if (entry?.listeners.length) {
      entry.listeners.forEach(cb => cb(data, error));
    }
  };

  return {
    load(key) {
      const entry = getEntry(key);
      const now = Date.now();

      if (entry.data && entry.expiry > now) {
        return Promise.resolve(entry.data);
      }

      if (entry.promise) {
        return entry.promise;
      }

      entry.promise = fetchFn(key)
        .then(data => {
          entry.data = data;
          entry.expiry = now + ttlMs;
          entry.promise = null;
          notify(key, data, null);
          return data;
        })
        .catch(err => {
          entry.promise = null;
          notify(key, null, err);
          throw err;
        });

      return entry.promise;
    },

    invalidate(key) {
      const entry = cache.get(key);
      if (entry) {
        entry.data = null;
        entry.expiry = 0;
        entry.promise = null;
      }
    },

    onChange(key, cb) {
      const entry = getEntry(key);
      entry.listeners.push(cb);
      return () => {
        entry.listeners = entry.listeners.filter(l => l !== cb);
      };
    }
  };
}
```

## Usage Example
```javascript
const loader = createDataLoader(fetchUser, 5000);

// Two different keys
loader.onChange('user:1', (data) => console.log('User 1 updated:', data));
loader.onChange('user:2', (data) => console.log('User 2 updated:', data));

// Concurrent loads for same key are deduplicated
loader.load('user:1').then(console.log);
loader.load('user:1').then(console.log); // Reuses in-flight request

// Invalidate and reload
loader.invalidate('user:1');
loader.load('user:1');
```

## Eviction Strategies
- **TTL-based**: Automatic expiration after ttlMs (implemented above)
- **LRU**: Evict least recently used entries when cache size exceeds limit
- **Manual**: Explicit invalidate() calls

## Error Handling
- Failed fetches propagate errors to all waiters via rejected Promise
- Listeners receive error state for reactive UI updates
- In-flight promises are cleared on error to allow retries

